<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nanda Bayin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1535">1535</a>–<a href="/years/1600">1600</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toungoo-dynasty">Toungoo dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nanda Bayin was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-toungoo-empire">First Toungoo Empire</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> from 1581 to 1599, the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a>. He inherited the largest empire in the history of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> but faced revolts, and his repeated failed campaigns against Siam under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naresuan">Naresuan</a>, which won its independence in 1584, exhausted the country. In 1599 the forces of Toungoo and Arakan took his capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a>, and he was killed in 1600.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nanda was born at the Toungoo Palace on 9 November 1535 to Princess Thakin Gyi and General Kyawhtin Nawrahta, the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a>, and was a grandson of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mingyi-nyo">Mingyi Nyo</a> and a nephew of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabinshwehti">Tabinshwehti</a>. He grew up mostly at the Pegu Palace and at 13 went with his father and uncle on their campaign in Siam, earning the title Minye Kyawswa for bravery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>. After Tabinshwehti's assassination in 1550 he fled with his mother and sister to his father, and when Bayinnaung took Toungoo on 11 January 1551 he made his 15-year-old son heir.</p><p>From 1557 Nanda took an ever larger part in his father's wars, becoming one of the king's four principal commanders with his uncles, and by the end of the reign he led whole campaigns himself. He also sat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> court, dominated by Mon ministers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Nanda succeeded without incident when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a> died on 10 October 1581 and was crowned on 15 October. The empire rested on his father's personal ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> rulers, and in the solar polity system each new high king had to win their loyalty again. A rebellion in the Shan states of Sanda and Thaungthut, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, was crushed in 1582–1583, but Ava and Siam rose in 1584. Ava was subdued; Siam was not.</p><p>Between 1584 and 1593 Nanda sent five invasions against Siam, all of which failed; his largest army, of 25,000 men in 1586, was about a third of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a>'s in 1568. He replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> with sons and loyalists where he could, but Mogaung revolted in 1590, and conscription drained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a>, where many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> fled to become monks, debt-slaves or refugees. Siamese forces besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> itself in 1594–1595, Prome revolted, and by 1597 every vassal had broken away.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Toungoo and Arakan agreed in late 1597 on a joint attack. The Arakanese navy, manned by Portuguese mercenaries, took Syriam in March 1599, and the allies besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a> from April. After his heir Minye Kyawswa II defected and was executed by Natshinnaung, Nanda surrendered on 19 December 1599 and was taken to Toungoo, where Natshinnaung, whom Nanda had once refused a marriage, assassinated him on 30 November 1600. Historians judge him an energetic king undone by an overextended empire without the administration or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> to hold it, and the Restored Toungoo reforms that lasted until 1885 grew out of his failures.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nanda Bayin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…(r. 1550–1581) had built the largest empire in the history of mainland Southeast Asia, but under his son <b>Nanda Bayin</b> it disintegrated: Siam broke free, Lower Burma was depopulated by war and famine, and in 1599 the capital Pegu was sacked by the forces of Toungoo and Arakan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/restored-toungoo-kingdom">Restored Toungoo Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Bayinnaung's death in 1581 the empire fell apart; Naresuan freed Ayutthaya in 1584, and <b>Nanda Bayin's</b> wars exhausted the realm.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-toungoo-empire">First Toungoo Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Taungoo, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. Died in Taungoo, in what is now <a href="/countries/myanmar">Myanmar</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=18.93,96.43&amp;year=1577" aria-label="Taungoo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7159 1462 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nanda Bayin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7609 1732h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7609 1732h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=18.93,96.43&amp;year=1577">What was here in 1577? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
